#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""bless -- the only path to a PyPI upload for this project.

    ./bless              run every gate, then ask: test, publish, or stop
    ./bless --branch-plan  print the branch plan as JSON and exit

No argument skips a stage: the gate runs first and it always runs, because
publishing is a single intent or it does not happen.  `--branch-plan` is not an
exception to that, because it does not enter the gate at all.  It answers one
question about this tree -- which branches `publish` would move, or why it
would refuse -- as a single JSON object on stdout, then exits: no index read,
no gates, no build, no prompt, no network, so it works in a tree whose `.venv`
is missing.  It exits 0 whenever it answers, including when the answer is a
refusal, because a refusal is data here and not a failure.  `bt` asks this
before it installs anything, and keeps its own copy of the policy for the
projects whose vendored `bless` is older than the flag.

When the gate passes, bless prints everything that is in hand and then prompts

    ====> Enter test or publish or quit:

and waits.  Exactly `test` rehearses the upload on TestPyPI, exactly `publish`
does the irreversible part, and nothing at all -- Enter, `quit`, or Ctrl-C --
stops with no tag, no upload and no push.  Any other answer asks again instead
of guessing, so a stray character can neither publish nor cancel by accident.

`test` is the rehearsal, aimed at the upload path itself.  The working version
is never spent on it: a throwaway `X.Y.Z.99.N` is built from a *copy* of the
tree, where 99 keeps it clear of every real release and N is one past whatever
TestPyPI already holds for that base (0 the first time).  It uploads only to
test.pypi.org, creates no tag and moves no branch, and it prints the pip line
that installs the result -- after a cache purge, because pip remembers an index
it has already read, and with `--extra-index-url` for PyPI, because TestPyPI
carries none of the dependencies.

The duplicate check is mandatory on every run, against the index the answer
would write to, and runs before anything is built or uploaded.  It refuses when
the version is already there, printing every published version in the refusal,
because an upload cannot be replaced.

The credential is looked up per target: `TWINE_PASSWORD` for PyPI,
`TWINE_TESTPYPI_PASSWORD` for TestPyPI, each overruling a `~/.pypirc` section
whose `repository` is that index's upload URL.  Whichever source answers, twine
is handed that one token as `TWINE_PASSWORD`, so a PyPI token is never spent on
TestPyPI just because both happen to be configured on the same machine.

Why no CI publish job: the upload is irreversible and the owner runs it from
the tree, so the safety has to be visible and local.  CI stays a gate.  The
branch model follows from the same decision: a release leaves every branch the
project names at one commit and tags it, so `publish` aligns them here instead
of asking the owner to remember.  `main` and `dev` is the default; `main` alone
is the supported model for a project that commits straight to the trunk.

How to reuse: this file is emitted verbatim by `build.py` in the `blessd`
repository and vendored at a project's root.  Edit it in `blessd` and re-emit;
never edit a project's copy, because the copy is what that project releases
with and nothing in the copy can tell you it has fallen behind.  `sha256sum
bless` matches a project's copy to the build it came from.  Nothing about the
project is written into it -- `identity()` reads the whole of it from
`pyproject.toml` at start-up:

  * the distribution name, used in the index URLs, the install line and every
    message, is `[project] name`;
  * the package that owns the release number is that name with `-` and `.`
    turned into `_`, overridable with `[tool.bless] module = "..."`;
  * the console script the smoke run starts is the first entry of
    `[project.scripts]`, and its name is all that is needed;
  * the inspector is `tests/inspect_dist.py`, overridable with
    `[tool.bless] inspect = "path"`, or `inspect = ""` to skip it knowingly;
  * `[tool.bless] smoke_args` adds an argv to that smoke run and
    `smoke_expect` is the text its output must contain;
  * `[tool.bless] branches` is the branch model `publish` keeps.  `["main",
    "dev"]`, the default, aligns the development branch with the trunk before
    tagging when that branch exists; `["main"]` releases from the trunk alone
    and aligns nothing.  One or two names and no more, because the plan
    fast-forwards one onto another.  A development branch that has not been
    created yet is not a refusal: while HEAD is the trunk, `bless` releases
    from the trunk and pushes only what exists.

What a project must then provide, because these are the assumptions that are
not worth guessing:

  * a flat layout -- the package directory at the root, since ROOT is the
    directory this file sits in;
  * `__version__ = "X.Y.Z"` in `<package>/__init__.py`, read by the build
    backend (flit does exactly this); bless rewrites that line only inside a
    throwaway copy, never in the tree;
  * `python -m build` working on the tree, and a dev extra holding ruff,
    pytest, pytest-cov, build, twine>=7 and packaging>=26, because `gates()`
    runs precisely ruff, compileall and pytest --cov=<package>.  twine is not
    made redundant by flit: `flit publish` rebuilds and sends whatever it just
    built, while bless sends *these two files* to the URL the answer targets,
    after `twine check --strict` has passed on them;
  * an inspector at that path: bless never uploads a distribution it has not
    inspected, so a missing one is a refusal, not a silent skip;
  * a console script, if declared, that prints the version for `--version`;
  * the branches named by `[tool.bless] branches` -- `main` and `dev` unless
    the project says otherwise -- with the trunk already pushed to origin; a
    development branch has to be pushed once it exists, and until it exists
    `bless` releases from the trunk alone; and `vX.Y.Z` tags.  The gate runs
    before the prompt, so it refuses a detached HEAD and a version that is
    already tagged: a rehearsal is always preceded by the checks the real
    release needs, never by looser ones.

Stages, in order: preflight (clean tree, tag absent, tool floors), index check,
gates (ruff, compileall, pytest with coverage), build into build/bless-<ver>/,
inspect the two files, `twine check --strict`, install-and-run smoke, the branch
plan.  Then the checklist and the prompt.  `test` builds the throwaway
`X.Y.Z.99.N` from a copy, uploads it and verifies it on TestPyPI, and stops
there.  `publish` aligns the release branches, tags, uploads to PyPI, verifies
and pushes.

Verifying means reading the index back, and that read is deliberately patient.
Both indexes answer through a CDN that has been taking minutes -- sometimes more
than ten -- to list a new release, so the read is cache-busted, it waits
VERIFY_BUDGET while printing how long it has waited, and it never calls an
accepted upload a failure: a release that is not listed yet is reported as lag,
with the project page to look at and what is left to do for the answer given.
"""

from __future__ import annotations

import argparse
import configparser
import contextlib
import hashlib
import importlib.util
import json
import os
import re
import shutil
import subprocess
import sys
import time
import urllib.error
import urllib.request
from importlib.metadata import PackageNotFoundError
from importlib.metadata import version as installed_version
from pathlib import Path

try:
    import tomllib
except ModuleNotFoundError:  # Python 3.10, which `requires-python` still allows
    import tomli as tomllib

ROOT = Path(__file__).resolve().parent
TESTPYPI_UPLOAD = 'https://test.pypi.org/legacy/'
INDEX_TIMEOUT = 15          # seconds for one index read
# Both indexes serve their JSON through a CDN, and since about 2026-10 a new
# release can take a quarter of an hour to appear in it.  So the post-upload
# read waits ~15 minutes in lengthening steps, and prints how long it has been
# waiting: a false "did not confirm" after a real upload is the worst answer
# this gate can give, because the upload cannot be taken back.
VERIFY_SCHEDULE = (3, 5, 5, 10, 10, 15, 15, 20, 20, 30, 30, 45, 45,
                   60, 60, 60, 60, 60, 60, 60, 60, 60, 60, 60)
VERIFY_BUDGET = sum(VERIFY_SCHEDULE)   # seconds, stated in every message
TEST_TAG = 99               # X.Y.Z.99.N sorts clear of every real release
# The one question the gate asks, spelled out in full because the answer that
# leaves the machine cannot be taken back.  The blank lines push it clear of the
# checklist, where the eye has just been reading columns.
MODE_PROMPT = '\n\n\n====> Enter test or publish or quit: '
TOOL_FLOORS = {'twine': (7, 0, 0), 'packaging': (26, 0, 0)}
# Why each tool is needed, said in the refusal: flit builds, but it uploads the
# files it just rebuilt, not two named files at a URL, and it has no standalone
# checker for a distribution it has already built.
TOOL_PURPOSE = {
    'twine': 'upload the two files the gate just inspected and to run '
             '`twine check --strict` on them',
    'packaging': 'read the metadata version that `twine check` validates',
}
DEFAULT_INSPECTOR = 'tests/inspect_dist.py'
# The branch model `publish` keeps, unless `[tool.bless] branches` says
# otherwise.  Two names mean "align the development branch with the trunk
# before tagging"; one name means "release from this branch, align nothing".
DEFAULT_BRANCHES = ('main', 'dev')
# The shape of `--branch-plan`'s report.  Bumped when a key changes meaning, so
# a caller that reads it can refuse a shape it was not written for instead of
# misreading one.
PLAN_SCHEMA = 1

# What a throwaway build must not copy out of the tree.
COPY_EXCLUDES = ('.git', 'build', 'dist', '.venv', 'env', 'venv', '__pycache__',
                 '.pytest_cache', '.ruff_cache', '.mypy_cache', 'htmlcov',
                 '.coverage*', '*.pyc')
VERSION_LINE = re.compile(r'^__version__ = .*$', re.MULTILINE)
VERSION_VALUE = re.compile(r"""^__version__ = ['"]([^'"]+)['"]""", re.MULTILINE)


class BlessError(RuntimeError):
    """A stage refused; the message is the whole explanation shown to the user."""


def say(stage: str, message: str) -> None:
    print(f'[{stage}] {message}', flush=True)


def _joined(branches) -> str:
    """'main' or 'main and dev': the branches as a statement reads them."""
    return ' and '.join(branches)


def _or_joined(branches) -> str:
    """'main' or 'main or dev': the branches as the only choices there are."""
    return ' or '.join(branches)


def _manifest(root: Path) -> dict:
    """This tree's pyproject.toml: the one place its identity is written down."""
    path = root / 'pyproject.toml'
    try:
        return tomllib.loads(path.read_text(encoding='utf-8'))
    except OSError as exc:
        raise BlessError(
            f'cannot read {path}: {exc}\nbless learns the project name, the '
            'package holding `__version__` and the console script from it, and '
            'will not guess any of them') from None


def _branches(declared) -> tuple[str, ...]:
    """`[tool.bless] branches`: the trunk alone, or the trunk and one more.

    Refusing anything else is deliberate.  `publish` fast-forwards one branch
    onto another, and a plan that quietly picked which of three branches to
    move would be a guess about the project's model instead of a gate on it.
    """
    if declared is None:
        return DEFAULT_BRANCHES
    if (not isinstance(declared, list) or not 1 <= len(declared) <= 2
            or len(set(declared)) != len(declared)
            or not all(isinstance(branch, str) and branch for branch in declared)):
        raise BlessError(
            f'[tool.bless] branches is {declared!r}; bless supports one branch '
            'name or two.\nUse ["main"] to release from the trunk alone, or '
            '["main", "dev"] (the default) to align a development branch with '
            'it before tagging.')
    return tuple(declared)


def identity(root: Path = ROOT) -> dict:
    """What bless must know about the project, read rather than written here.

    `[project] name` gives the distribution name; the package holding the
    release number is that name with `-` and `.` turned into `_`; the console
    script is the first entry of `[project.scripts]`; `[tool.bless]` overrides
    any of it, including the branch model `publish` keeps.  Refusing here -- and
    naming both ways to fix it -- is what lets the script move to another
    project without guessing wrong in silence.
    """
    manifest = _manifest(root)
    project = manifest.get('project') or {}
    settings = (manifest.get('tool') or {}).get('bless') or {}
    name = project.get('name')
    if not name:
        raise BlessError(
            f'{root / "pyproject.toml"} has no [project] name, which bless needs '
            'for the index URLs, the install line and every message')
    module = settings.get('module') or re.sub(r'[-.]+', '_', name)
    package = root / module / '__init__.py'
    if not package.is_file():
        raise BlessError(
            f'[project] name is {name!r}, so bless reads the release number from '
            f'{module}/__init__.py, but {package.relative_to(root)} is missing.\n'
            'Put the package and its `__version__` line there, or name the package '
            'that holds them:\n  [tool.bless]\n  module = "the_package"')
    if not VERSION_LINE.search(package.read_text(encoding='utf-8')):
        raise BlessError(
            f'{package.relative_to(root)} declares no `__version__ = ...`; bless '
            'takes the release number from there and refuses to invent one')
    scripts = list(project.get('scripts') or {})
    return {
        'root': root,
        'name': name,
        'module': module,
        'console': scripts[0] if scripts else None,
        'inspector': settings.get('inspect', DEFAULT_INSPECTOR),
        'smoke_args': list(settings.get('smoke_args') or []),
        'smoke_expect': settings.get('smoke_expect'),
        'branches': _branches(settings.get('branches')),
    }


try:
    PROJECT = identity()
except BlessError as complaint:
    print(f'bless: {complaint}', file=sys.stderr)
    raise SystemExit(1) from None

NAME = PROJECT['name']
MODULE = PROJECT['module']
# PEP 503 normalization: the spelling the project pages use, whatever the
# distribution name does with `-`, `_` and `.`.
NORMALIZED = re.sub(r'[-_.]+', '-', NAME).lower()


def read_version(root: Path = ROOT, module: str = MODULE) -> str:
    """The release number, read out of the package instead of importing it.

    Reading the line keeps bless working on a GUI package without starting one,
    and keeps it working in a tree that was never installed.
    """
    package = root / module / '__init__.py'
    match = VERSION_VALUE.search(package.read_text(encoding='utf-8'))
    if match is None:
        raise BlessError(
            f'{package.relative_to(root)} has no quoted `__version__ = ...` line; '
            'bless takes the release number from there and refuses to invent one')
    return match.group(1)


# The two destinations.  `upload` is None for PyPI because that is twine's own
# default end point; `pypirc` is the repository URL a ~/.pypirc section has to
# name before its credential is used for this index; `token` is the environment
# variable that wins over the file; `page` is where a human can see the release
# while the JSON is still catching up.  A TestPyPI token is not a PyPI token, so
# the two are kept apart rather than merged.
PYPI_URL = f'https://pypi.org/pypi/{NAME}/json'
TESTPYPI_URL = f'https://test.pypi.org/pypi/{NAME}/json'
PYPI = {'name': 'PyPI', 'index': PYPI_URL, 'upload': None,
        'pypirc': 'https://upload.pypi.org/legacy/',
        'token': 'TWINE_PASSWORD',
        'page': f'https://pypi.org/project/{NORMALIZED}/'}
TESTPYPI = {'name': 'TestPyPI', 'index': TESTPYPI_URL, 'upload': TESTPYPI_UPLOAD,
            'pypirc': TESTPYPI_UPLOAD,
            'token': 'TWINE_TESTPYPI_PASSWORD',
            'page': f'https://test.pypi.org/project/{NORMALIZED}/'}


def run(command: list[str], *, capture: bool = False,
        env: dict[str, str] | None = None) -> subprocess.CompletedProcess:
    """Run a command from the repository root, capturing when asked."""
    return subprocess.run(  # noqa: S603 - argv lists built here, never a shell string
        [str(part) for part in command],
        cwd=ROOT,
        env=env,
        text=True,
        stdout=subprocess.PIPE if capture else None,
        stderr=subprocess.PIPE if capture else None,
        check=False,
    )


def git(*arguments: str) -> str:
    result = run(['git', *arguments], capture=True)
    if result.returncode != 0:
        raise BlessError(f'git {" ".join(arguments)} failed: {result.stderr.strip()}')
    return result.stdout.strip()


def _version_tuple(text: str) -> tuple[int, int, int]:
    parts = tuple(int(part) for part in re.findall(r'\d+', text)[:3])
    return (parts + (0, 0, 0))[:3]


def _tool_floor(name: str, minimum: tuple[int, int, int]) -> None:
    """One tool this gate cannot do without; the message says what it is for."""
    try:
        found = installed_version(name)
    except PackageNotFoundError:
        raise BlessError(
            f'{name} is not installed, and bless uses it to '
            f'{TOOL_PURPOSE.get(name, "run this gate")}.\n'
            f'bless checks the interpreter it runs under ({sys.executable}), so '
            'install the dev extra into that one:\n'
            '  python -m pip install -e ".[dev]"\n'
            '  flit install --extras dev          # if you install with flit') from None
    if _version_tuple(found) < minimum:
        wanted = '.'.join(str(part) for part in minimum)
        raise BlessError(
            f'{name} {found} is older than the required {wanted}; '
            f'the validator must be at least as new as the metadata writer')


def preflight(version: str) -> dict:
    """Refuse to start unless the tree and the toolchain can be trusted.

    The check on the tree is the one that decides what gets built: it has to
    describe a commit.  The branch and the tag are checked here as well because
    the prompt may end in a tag and a push, and that question is asked only
    after this has passed -- one gate, not a gentle one and a strict one.
    """
    status = git('status', '--porcelain')
    if status:
        raise BlessError(
            'the working tree is not clean, so what gets built would not '
            f'describe a commit:\n{status}')

    branch = git('rev-parse', '--abbrev-ref', 'HEAD')
    if branch == 'HEAD':
        raise BlessError(
            'HEAD is detached; bless publishes only from '
            f'{_or_joined(PROJECT["branches"])}')
    sha = git('rev-parse', '--short', 'HEAD')
    if run(['git', 'rev-parse', '-q', '--verify', f'refs/tags/v{version}'],
           capture=True).returncode == 0:
        raise BlessError(f'the tag v{version} already exists locally')

    for tool, minimum in TOOL_FLOORS.items():
        _tool_floor(tool, minimum)
    if run([sys.executable, '-m', 'build', '--version'], capture=True).returncode != 0:
        raise BlessError('python -m build is unavailable; install the dev extra')

    return {'branch': branch, 'sha': sha}


def _read_json(url: str, *, bust: bool = False) -> tuple[dict | None, str]:
    """GET one JSON document: `(document, '')`, or `(None, complaint)`.

    A 404 comes back as `(None, '')`, because it means two different things and
    only the caller knows which: before a project's first upload it is an empty
    index, and after one it is a cache that has not caught up.

    `bust=True` adds a unique query string and refuses any cached copy.  Both
    indexes are served through a CDN, where a fresh 404 -- or a `releases` map
    without the release that was just accepted -- can sit for a quarter of an
    hour; re-reading the URL the pre-flight check already cached would spend the
    whole wait looking at the wrong answer.
    """
    base = url
    if bust:
        url = f'{base}?bless={time.time_ns()}'
    request = urllib.request.Request(
        url, headers={'Accept': 'application/json',
                      'Cache-Control': 'no-cache', 'Pragma': 'no-cache'})
    try:
        with urllib.request.urlopen(request, timeout=INDEX_TIMEOUT) as response:  # noqa: S310
            document = json.load(response)
    except urllib.error.HTTPError as exc:
        return None, '' if exc.code == 404 else f'{base}: {exc}'
    except (urllib.error.URLError, OSError, ValueError) as exc:
        return None, f'{base}: {exc}'
    if not isinstance(document, dict) or not isinstance(document.get('releases'), dict):
        return None, f'{base} did not return a releases map'
    return document, ''


def fetch_index(target: dict = PYPI) -> dict:
    """The JSON document for this project on `target`, or a refusal.

    A 404 is not a failure here: it is what either index answers before the
    project has its first upload, and the duplicate check then has nothing to
    collide with.
    """
    document, complaint = _read_json(target['index'])
    if complaint:
        raise BlessError(
            f'cannot read {complaint}\n'
            'the duplicate check is mandatory on every run, so bless stops '
            'here')
    if document is None:
        say('index', f'{target["index"]} has no project yet (404); '
                     'reading it as empty')
        return {'releases': {}}
    return document


def _publication_key(text: str) -> tuple[int, tuple[int, ...]]:
    try:
        return (0, tuple(int(part) for part in text.split('.')))
    except ValueError:
        return (1, ())


def published_versions(document: dict) -> list[str]:
    return sorted(document['releases'], key=_publication_key)


def next_test_version(version: str, document: dict) -> str:
    """`2.0.0` becomes `2.0.0.99.0`, or one past the highest suffix in use.

    Only suffixes of *this* base version count, so a rehearsal of an older
    release never pushes the next one's counter forward, and a project that
    TestPyPI has never seen starts at 0.
    """
    pattern = re.compile(rf'^{re.escape(version)}\.{TEST_TAG}\.(\d+)$')
    used = [int(match.group(1)) for name in document['releases']
            if (match := pattern.match(name))]
    return f'{version}.{TEST_TAG}.{max(used, default=-1) + 1}'


def check_index(version: str, document: dict | None = None,
                target: dict = PYPI) -> list[str]:
    """Refuse when `version` is already on `target`; return the published list.

    Runs on every pass of the gate, and again for the throwaway version before a
    rehearsal sends it: finding out that the version exists must not require
    building the artifact first, and the refusal names every version that index
    already has.
    """
    document = fetch_index(target) if document is None else document
    releases = document['releases']
    published = published_versions(document)
    if str(version) in releases:
        listing = ', '.join(published) if published else '(none)'
        raise BlessError(
            f'{NAME} {version} is already on {target["name"]}, and an upload '
            f'cannot be replaced.\n  published versions: {listing}\n'
            f'  bump __version__ in {MODULE}/__init__.py, commit, and bless again')
    latest = published[-1] if published else 'none'
    say('index', f'{NAME} {version} is not on {target["name"]} '
                 f'({len(published)} versions exist; latest is {latest})')
    return published


def gates() -> None:
    """The same three gates CI runs, on the same interpreter."""
    compiled = [MODULE, *(name for name in ('conftest.py', 'tests')
                          if (ROOT / name).exists())]
    steps = [
        ('lint', [sys.executable, '-m', 'ruff', 'check', '.']),
        ('compile', [sys.executable, '-m', 'compileall', '-q', *compiled]),
        ('tests', [sys.executable, '-m', 'pytest', '-q',
                   f'--cov={MODULE}', '--cov-report=term-missing']),
    ]
    for name, command in steps:
        say('gate', name)
        result = run(command)
        if result.returncode != 0:
            raise BlessError(f'the {name} gate failed (exit {result.returncode})')


def _artifacts(outdir: Path) -> tuple[Path, Path]:
    """Exactly one wheel and one sdist, or a refusal naming what was found."""
    wheels = sorted(outdir.glob('*.whl'))
    sdists = sorted(outdir.glob('*.tar.gz'))
    if len(wheels) != 1 or len(sdists) != 1:
        raise BlessError(
            f'expected exactly one wheel and one sdist in '
            f'{outdir.relative_to(ROOT)}, found '
            f'{[path.name for path in wheels + sdists]}')
    return wheels[0], sdists[0]


def build(version: str) -> tuple[Path, Path]:
    """Build into a fresh directory so nothing older can be uploaded."""
    outdir = ROOT / 'build' / f'bless-{version}'
    if outdir.exists():
        shutil.rmtree(outdir)
    say('build', f'{outdir.relative_to(ROOT)} (wiped first)')
    result = run([sys.executable, '-m', 'build', '--outdir', str(outdir)])
    if result.returncode != 0:
        raise BlessError(f'python -m build failed (exit {result.returncode})')
    return _artifacts(outdir)


def _set_version(root: Path, version: str) -> None:
    """Rewrite the one `__version__` line in a *copy* of this tree.

    The module name is the one resolved at start-up, because the copy *is* this
    tree: same layout, same `[project]` table, different version.
    """
    path = root / MODULE / '__init__.py'
    text = path.read_text(encoding='utf-8')
    updated, count = VERSION_LINE.subn(f'__version__ = {version!r}', text)
    if count != 1:
        raise BlessError(
            f'expected exactly one `__version__ = ...` line in '
            f'{path.relative_to(root)}, found {count}; a throwaway version '
            'cannot be built from this tree')
    path.write_text(updated, encoding='utf-8')


def build_for_test(version: str) -> tuple[Path, Path]:
    """Build the throwaway version from a copy, leaving the real tree alone.

    The backend reads `__version__` from the module, so a version that is not
    the working one needs its own copy.  Editing the tree instead would dirty
    it, spend the version it is holding, and make the next tag describe
    something that was never committed.
    """
    work = ROOT / 'build' / f'bless-test-{version}'
    if work.exists():
        shutil.rmtree(work)
    source = work / 'src'
    say('build', f'{source.relative_to(ROOT)} (a copy, __version__ = {version})')
    shutil.copytree(ROOT, source, ignore=shutil.ignore_patterns(*COPY_EXCLUDES))
    _set_version(source, version)

    outdir = work / 'dist'
    result = run([sys.executable, '-m', 'build', '--outdir', str(outdir),
                  str(source)])
    if result.returncode != 0:
        raise BlessError(f'python -m build failed (exit {result.returncode})')
    return _artifacts(outdir)


def inspector():
    """Load the inspector CI also uses, so the two checks cannot drift."""
    where = PROJECT['inspector']
    if not where:
        raise BlessError(
            '[tool.bless] inspect is empty, so there is nothing to inspect with')
    path = ROOT / where
    if not path.is_file():
        raise BlessError(
            f'{where} is missing, and bless never uploads a distribution it has '
            'not inspected.\nAdd the inspector, point [tool.bless] inspect at '
            'yours, or set inspect = "" to skip it knowingly.')
    spec = importlib.util.spec_from_file_location('inspect_dist', path)
    module = importlib.util.module_from_spec(spec)
    spec.loader.exec_module(module)
    return module


def inspect_dist(wheel: Path, sdist: Path, version: str | None = None) -> None:
    """Assert the artifacts against the tree; `version` is the rehearsal's own."""
    if not PROJECT['inspector']:
        say('inspect', 'skipped: [tool.bless] inspect is empty')
        return
    say('inspect', f'{wheel.name} and {sdist.name} against {PROJECT["inspector"]}')
    try:
        inspector().check_all(wheel, sdist, version)
    except ValueError as exc:
        raise BlessError(str(exc)) from None


def check(wheel: Path, sdist: Path) -> None:
    say('check', 'twine check --strict')
    result = run([sys.executable, '-m', 'twine', 'check', '--strict',
                  str(wheel), str(sdist)])
    if result.returncode != 0:
        raise BlessError(
            'twine check --strict failed. If it rejects a Metadata 2.5 field, '
            'pin the build backend to flit_core<4 and bless again')


def smoke(wheel: Path, version: str) -> None:
    """Install the built wheel in a throwaway venv and run what it installed.

    `[project.scripts]` decides what runs: the first console script, if the
    project declares one, otherwise the package itself.  Either way the version
    has to come back out, which is why the docstring calls that a requirement.
    """
    venv = wheel.parent / 'smoke-venv'
    if venv.exists():
        shutil.rmtree(venv)
    say('smoke', f'installing {wheel.name} into {venv.relative_to(ROOT)}')
    for step in ([sys.executable, '-m', 'venv', str(venv)],
                 [str(venv / 'bin' / 'python'), '-m', 'pip', 'install',
                  '--quiet', '--upgrade', 'pip'],
                 [str(venv / 'bin' / 'python'), '-m', 'pip', 'install',
                  '--quiet', str(wheel)]):
        if run(step).returncode != 0:
            raise BlessError(f'smoke install failed: {" ".join(step[1:])}')

    script = venv / 'bin' / PROJECT['console'] if PROJECT['console'] else None
    reported = run(
        [script, '--version'] if script else
        [venv / 'bin' / 'python', '-c',
         f'import {MODULE}; print({MODULE}.__version__)'],
        capture=True)
    if reported.returncode != 0 or version not in reported.stdout:
        raise BlessError(
            f'the installed package did not report {version}: '
            f'{reported.stdout.strip() or reported.stderr.strip()}\n'
            'bless requires a declared console script to print the version '
            'for --version')
    say('smoke', f'installed wheel reports {NAME} {version}')

    if script and PROJECT['smoke_args']:
        wanted = PROJECT['smoke_expect'] or ''
        command = [script, *PROJECT['smoke_args']]
        listing = run(command, capture=True)
        if listing.returncode != 0 or wanted not in listing.stdout:
            raise BlessError(
                f'the installed console script failed '
                f'{" ".join(PROJECT["smoke_args"])}: '
                f'{listing.stdout.strip() or listing.stderr.strip()}')
        say('smoke', f'{PROJECT["console"]} {" ".join(PROJECT["smoke_args"])} '
                     f'printed {wanted!r}')


def branch_plan(current: str | None, tips: dict[str, str | None], is_ancestor,
                branches: tuple[str, ...] = DEFAULT_BRANCHES) -> dict:
    """Decide what `publish` must do to leave the release branches identical.

    `branches` is `[tool.bless] branches`: one name for a project that commits
    straight to the trunk, two for a project with a development branch.  Pure in
    everything that matters -- the caller supplies the tips and the ancestry
    test, so the whole table in the plan is testable without a git remote.
    Returns `{'refusal': reason}`, or `{'align': branch|None, 'release':
    branches}`, where `align` names the single branch that has to catch up and
    `release` is what will be pushed.

    A development branch that does not exist yet is not a refusal.  The model
    says "align the trunk with the development branch *if there is one*", and
    demanding one would make `branches = ["main", "dev"]` mean "you may not
    release until you have created `dev`".  Releasing from the trunk alone is
    allowed exactly while HEAD is the trunk -- a development branch that is
    missing cannot be the branch being blessed -- and then `release` holds the
    trunk only, so nothing absent is ever pushed.
    """
    if current not in branches:
        where = 'a detached HEAD' if current is None else f'branch {current}'
        return {'refusal': (
            f'HEAD is on {where}; bless publishes only from '
            f'{_or_joined(branches)}')}

    trunk, *development = branches
    if not tips.get(trunk):
        return {'refusal': f'{trunk} does not exist locally'}
    if not tips.get(f'origin/{trunk}'):
        return {'refusal': f'origin/{trunk} does not exist; push it before blessing'}

    present = [trunk]
    for branch in development:
        if not tips.get(branch):
            if current != trunk:
                return {'refusal': (
                    f'{current} is checked out but does not exist locally, so '
                    f'bless cannot align {trunk} with it')}
            continue
        if not tips.get(f'origin/{branch}'):
            return {'refusal': (
                f'origin/{branch} does not exist; push it before blessing')}
        present.append(branch)

    follower = next((branch for branch in present if branch != current), None)
    align = None
    if follower is not None and tips[follower] != tips[current]:
        if is_ancestor(tips[follower], tips[current]):
            align = follower
        elif is_ancestor(tips[current], tips[follower]):
            align = current
        else:
            return {'refusal': (
                f'{current} ({tips[current][:7]}) and {follower} '
                f'({tips[follower][:7]}) have diverged; merge or rebase until '
                'one contains the other, then bless again')}

    # Afterwards every branch sits at one commit, and origin has to be able to
    # fast-forward to it -- checked here, because the push happens last, after
    # the upload, when a refusal can no longer be undone.
    target = tips[follower] if align == current else tips[current]
    for branch in present:
        if not is_ancestor(tips[f'origin/{branch}'], target):
            return {'refusal': (
                f'pushing {branch} would not be a fast-forward on origin; '
                f'pull/rebase {branch} first')}
    return {'align': align, 'release': tuple(present)}


def _maybe_rev(reference: str) -> str | None:
    result = run(['git', 'rev-parse', '--verify', '-q', reference], capture=True)
    return result.stdout.strip() if result.returncode == 0 else None


def branch_stage(branch: str,
                 branches: tuple[str, ...] = DEFAULT_BRANCHES) -> dict:
    names = [*branches, *(f'origin/{name}' for name in branches)]
    tips = {name: _maybe_rev(name) for name in names}
    plan = branch_plan(
        branch, tips,
        lambda older, newer: run(
            ['git', 'merge-base', '--is-ancestor', older, newer],
            capture=True).returncode == 0,
        branches)
    if 'refusal' in plan:
        raise BlessError(plan['refusal'])
    released = plan['release']
    if plan['align']:
        say('branch', f'would fast-forward {plan["align"]} to {tips[branch][:7]}')
    elif len(released) > 1:
        say('branch', f'{_joined(released)} are already the same commit')
    elif len(branches) > 1:
        say('branch', f'{branches[1]} does not exist; releasing from '
                      f'{released[0]} alone')
    else:
        say('branch', f'{released[0]} is the only release branch')
    return plan


def _plan_state(current: str | None, tips: dict[str, str | None], plan: dict,
                branches: tuple[str, ...], diverged: bool) -> str:
    """Which row of the plan table this is, in one word a caller can switch on.

    `branch_plan` stays the authority -- this only names what it decided, so a
    caller can choose its own sentences and its own severity without parsing
    the refusal text.  `diverged` is the one fact the plan does not carry
    separately, because it only ever appears inside a refusal.
    """
    if current is None:
        return 'detached'
    if current not in branches:
        return 'off-branch'
    trunk, *development = branches
    if not tips.get(trunk):
        return 'no-trunk'
    if 'refusal' in plan:
        if diverged:
            return 'diverged'
        if any(tips.get(name) and not tips.get(f'origin/{name}')
               for name in branches):
            return 'unpushed'
        return 'refused'
    if plan['align']:
        return 'align'
    if development and not tips.get(development[0]):
        return 'missing-dev'
    if len(plan['release']) > 1:
        return 'same'
    return 'single'


def plan_report() -> dict:
    """`--branch-plan`: the branch plan as data, and nothing else.

    The report is the same `branch_plan()` table `publish` obeys, annotated
    with the facts a caller needs to say it in its own words: the tips of every
    ref the model names, the branches that exist but are not on origin, and
    `state`, one word naming which row of that table this is.
    """
    branches = PROJECT['branches']
    branch = git('rev-parse', '--abbrev-ref', 'HEAD')
    current = None if branch == 'HEAD' else branch
    names = [*branches, *(f'origin/{name}' for name in branches)]
    tips = {name: _maybe_rev(name) for name in names}

    def is_ancestor(older: str, newer: str) -> bool:
        return run(['git', 'merge-base', '--is-ancestor', older, newer],
                   capture=True).returncode == 0

    plan = branch_plan(current, tips, is_ancestor, branches)
    trunk, *development = branches
    second = development[0] if development else None
    diverged = bool(second and tips.get(trunk) and tips.get(second)
                    and tips[trunk] != tips[second]
                    and not is_ancestor(tips[trunk], tips[second])
                    and not is_ancestor(tips[second], tips[trunk]))
    return {
        'schema': PLAN_SCHEMA,
        'project': NAME,
        'branch': current,
        'branches': list(branches),
        'tips': tips,
        'unpushed': [name for name in branches
                     if tips.get(name) and not tips.get(f'origin/{name}')],
        'state': _plan_state(current, tips, plan, branches, diverged),
        'reason': plan.get('refusal'),
        'align': plan.get('align'),
        'release': list(plan.get('release') or ()),
    }


def align_branches(plan: dict, branch: str) -> None:
    if not plan['align']:
        return
    target = plan['align']
    git('branch', '-f', target, branch)
    say('branch', f'fast-forwarded {target} to {branch}')


def tag(version: str) -> None:
    git('tag', f'v{version}')
    say('tag', f'v{version}')


def untag(version: str) -> None:
    with contextlib.suppress(BlessError):
        git('tag', '-d', f'v{version}')


def _pypirc_credentials(target: dict,
                        path: Path | None = None) -> tuple[str, str] | None:
    """The username and password `~/.pypirc` holds for `target`, if any.

    twine only consults that file for a section *name*, and bless uploads by
    URL, so the lookup happens here: a section counts when its `repository` is
    this index's upload URL.  A missing or unreadable file simply answers None.
    """
    path = Path.home() / '.pypirc' if path is None else path
    if not path.is_file():
        return None
    config = configparser.RawConfigParser()
    try:
        config.read(path)
    except (OSError, configparser.Error):
        return None
    wanted = target['pypirc'].rstrip('/')
    for section in config.sections():
        if config[section].get('repository', '').rstrip('/') != wanted:
            continue
        password = config[section].get('password')
        if password:
            return config[section].get('username') or '__token__', password
    return None


def upload(wheel: Path, sdist: Path, version: str, target: dict = PYPI) -> None:
    """Send the two named files to `target` with that index's own credential."""
    where, token = target['name'], target['token']
    env = dict(os.environ)
    env.setdefault('TWINE_USERNAME', '__token__')
    if env.get(token):
        env['TWINE_PASSWORD'] = env[token]
        source = f'the {token} environment variable'
    else:
        found = _pypirc_credentials(target)
        if found is None:
            raise BlessError(
                f'{token} is not set and ~/.pypirc has no section whose '
                f'repository is {target["pypirc"]}, so there is no credential '
                f'for {where}.\nA {where} token is not a token for the other '
                'index.  Either export it:\n'
                f'  export {token}=pypi-...\n'
                'or add a section to ~/.pypirc:\n'
                f'  [{where.lower()}]\n'
                f'  repository = {target["pypirc"]}\n'
                '  username = __token__\n  password = pypi-...')
        env['TWINE_USERNAME'], env['TWINE_PASSWORD'] = found
        source = f'the ~/.pypirc section for {where}'
    command = [sys.executable, '-m', 'twine', 'upload']
    if target['upload']:
        command += ['--repository-url', target['upload']]
    say('upload', f'{wheel.name} and {sdist.name} to {where} using {source}')
    result = run([*command, str(wheel), str(sdist)], env=env)
    if result.returncode != 0:
        if target['upload']:
            raise BlessError(
                f'twine upload to {where} failed; nothing was published and the '
                'tree is untouched.\nIf that index rejected a Metadata 2.5 '
                'field, pin the build backend to flit_core<4, rebuild, and '
                'bless again.')
        untag(version)
        raise BlessError(
            'twine upload failed; the local tag was removed and nothing was '
            'pushed.\nIf PyPI rejected a Metadata 2.5 field, pin the build '
            'backend to flit_core<4, rebuild, and bless again.')


def _metadata_mismatches(version: str, wheel: Path, document: dict) -> list[str]:
    releases = document['releases']
    info = document.get('info', {})
    project = inspector().project()
    problems: list[str] = []

    files = releases.get(version) or []
    local_sha = hashlib.sha256(Path(wheel).read_bytes()).hexdigest()
    digests = [entry.get('digests', {}).get('sha256')
               for entry in files if entry.get('packagetype') == 'bdist_wheel']
    if not digests or digests[0] != local_sha:
        problems.append('the index wheel SHA256 differs from the uploaded file')

    if info.get('summary') != project['description']:
        problems.append(
            f'summary: expected {project["description"]!r}, '
            f'found {info.get("summary")!r}')
    if info.get('requires_python') != project['requires-python']:
        problems.append(
            f'requires_python: expected {project["requires-python"]!r}, '
            f'found {info.get("requires_python")!r}')

    # The index reports the dev extra too, with an `extra == "dev"` marker.
    expected = inspector().runtime_names(project['dependencies'])
    found = inspector().runtime_names(info.get('requires_dist') or [])
    if found != expected:
        problems.append(
            f'Requires-Dist: expected {sorted(expected)}, found {sorted(found)}')

    wanted_keywords = set(project.get('keywords', []))
    found_keywords = {part.strip() for part in (info.get('keywords') or '').split(',')
                      if part.strip()}
    if found_keywords != wanted_keywords:
        problems.append(
            f'keywords: expected {sorted(wanted_keywords)}, '
            f'found {sorted(found_keywords)}')
    return problems


def _index_lag(version: str, wheel: Path, document: dict) -> str:
    """What the index still lacks; an empty string means it has caught up.

    The `info` block describes the *latest* release, so it is only compared once
    it has moved on to this version -- before that it is the previous release's
    summary and keywords, and complaining about them would be a false negative
    during exactly the window this loop exists to wait out.
    """
    if str(version) not in document['releases']:
        listed = published_versions(document)
        recent = ', '.join(listed[-3:]) if listed else 'no releases at all'
        return f'the project is listed but this release is not (it has {recent})'
    described = (document.get('info') or {}).get('version')
    if described != str(version):
        return ('the release is listed, but the info block still describes '
                f'{described!r}')
    return '; '.join(_metadata_mismatches(version, wheel, document))


def verify_index(version: str, wheel: Path, target: dict = PYPI,
                 *, advice: str = '') -> None:
    """Read the index back until it serves this upload, or say why it will not.

    The wait is long on purpose (VERIFY_BUDGET).  Calling an accepted upload a
    failure because a CDN was slow is the one wrong answer available here: the
    files are already there, so the message has to say so and name what is left
    to do, which is `advice` from whoever asked for the read.
    """
    where = target['name']
    page = f'{target["page"]}{version}/'
    say('verify', f'waiting up to {VERIFY_BUDGET // 60} minutes for {where} to '
                  'list the upload (both indexes have been slow to do so; '
                  'Ctrl-C is safe here, the files are already sent)')
    lag = 'the index has not been read yet'
    waited = 0
    note = 30
    for pause in (0, *VERIFY_SCHEDULE):
        if pause:
            time.sleep(pause)
            waited += pause
        document, complaint = _read_json(target['index'], bust=True)
        if complaint:
            lag = f'cannot read the index: {complaint.splitlines()[0]}'
        elif document is None:
            lag = 'the JSON API still answers 404 for the whole project'
        else:
            remaining = _index_lag(version, wheel, document)
            if not remaining:
                say('verify', f'{where} serves {version} with matching metadata '
                              f'({waited}s after the upload)')
                return
            lag = remaining
        if waited >= note:
            say('verify', f'{waited}s: {lag}')
            note *= 2
    raise BlessError(
        f'{where} did not list {NAME} {version} within {VERIFY_BUDGET // 60} '
        f'minutes of reading the index back.\nLast read: {lag}.\n'
        'twine reported the upload as accepted, and an accepted upload cannot '
        'be withdrawn, so do not send this version again: what is missing is '
        'the index catching up, not the release.\n'
        f'  project page: {page}\n'
        f'  JSON to agree with: {target["index"]}'
        + (f'\n{advice}' if advice else ''))


def install_hint(version: str) -> None:
    """How to try the rehearsal build.

    TestPyPI deliberately serves no dependencies, so the command has to fall
    back to PyPI for those; and pip caches the index it has already read, so the
    cache goes first or the new file will not be seen.
    """
    print()
    print(f'{NAME} {version} is on TestPyPI: not on PyPI, and nothing was '
          'tagged.')
    print('Purge the index pip has cached, then install:')
    print()
    print('  python -m pip cache purge')
    print('  python -m pip install --no-cache-dir \\')
    print('      --index-url https://test.pypi.org/simple/ \\')
    print('      --extra-index-url https://pypi.org/simple/ \\')
    print(f'      {NAME}=={version}')
    print()
    print('The extra index is required: TestPyPI carries none of the '
          'dependencies.')
    print(f'Undo it with: python -m pip uninstall {NAME}')


def push_references(branch: str, version: str,
                    branches: tuple[str, ...] | None = None) -> list[str]:
    """What `publish` pushes: the branch HEAD is on, the others, then the tag.

    `branches` is the plan's `release` list, so a development branch that does
    not exist yet is never pushed, and `PROJECT['branches']` stays the default
    only for a caller that has no plan.
    """
    branches = PROJECT['branches'] if branches is None else branches
    others = [name for name in branches if name != branch]
    return [branch, *others, f'v{version}']


def publish_git(references: list[str]) -> None:
    """Push each reference; a refusal names the whole push, not the one step."""
    for reference in references:
        result = run(['git', 'push', 'origin', reference])
        if result.returncode != 0:
            raise BlessError(
                f'git push origin {reference} failed; the index is already '
                'correct.\nFinish the job with:\n'
                f'  git push origin {" ".join(references)}')
        say('push', reference)


def checklist(version: str, facts: dict, public: list[str],
              wheel: Path, sdist: Path, plan: dict) -> None:
    """What is in hand, printed before the prompt asks what to do with it."""
    branches = plan.get('release') or PROJECT['branches']
    others = [name for name in branches if name != facts['branch']]
    if plan['align']:
        align = f'fast-forward {plan["align"]} to {facts["sha"]}'
    elif others:
        align = f'nothing ({_joined(branches)} already match)'
    else:
        align = f'nothing ({branches[0]} is the only release branch)'
    latest = public[-1] if public else 'none'
    print()
    print('Every gate passed, and nothing has been tagged, uploaded or pushed.')
    print(f'  tree       clean at {facts["sha"]} on {facts["branch"]}')
    print('  gates      ruff, compileall, pytest --cov all passed')
    print(f'  index      {version} is not published (latest is {latest})')
    print(f'  artifacts  {wheel.relative_to(ROOT)}')
    print(f'             {sdist.relative_to(ROOT)}')
    print('  checks     inspect_dist, twine check --strict, smoke install passed')
    print('  branches   ' + align)
    print(f'  would tag  v{version}')
    print(f'  would send {wheel.name} and {sdist.name} to PyPI')
    print(f'  would push {", ".join([facts["branch"], *others])} and v{version} '
          'to origin')
    print()
    print('  test      build a throwaway X.Y.Z.99.N from a copy and upload it')
    print('            to TestPyPI; no tag, no branch, no push')
    print('  publish   tag, upload to PyPI and push -- everything above, for')
    print('            real, and it cannot be taken back')
    print('  quit      stop here; pressing Enter does the same')


def rehearse(version: str) -> None:
    """Answering `test`: upload `X.Y.Z.99.N` to TestPyPI, touch no git state.

    The gate has already run and passed -- same tree checks, same three gates,
    same inspector, same smoke install -- so what this adds is the upload path
    itself, aimed at test.pypi.org under its own token.  What it proves is that
    the sending works, not that a second, looser path exists.
    """
    document = fetch_index(TESTPYPI)
    candidate = next_test_version(version, document)
    check_index(candidate, document, TESTPYPI)
    wheel, sdist = build_for_test(candidate)
    inspect_dist(wheel, sdist, candidate)
    check(wheel, sdist)
    smoke(wheel, candidate)
    upload(wheel, sdist, candidate, TESTPYPI)
    verify_index(candidate, wheel, TESTPYPI, advice=(
        'Nothing is stuck: this build is spent, and the next ./bless run, '
        'answered test, uploads the next .99.N.'))
    install_hint(candidate)


def ask_mode() -> str:
    """What to do with a tree that passed: 'test', 'publish', or '' to stop.

    The word has to be typed out in full.  `test` and `publish` are one
    keystroke away from nothing at all, and this is the only question in the
    gate whose answer cannot be undone, so nothing is inferred from a prefix.
    Nothing typed, `quit` and Ctrl-C all mean stop; anything else asks again,
    which means a stray character can neither publish nor cancel.
    """
    while True:
        try:
            answer = input(MODE_PROMPT).strip()
        except (EOFError, KeyboardInterrupt):
            print()
            return ''
        if answer in ('', 'quit'):
            return ''
        if answer in ('test', 'publish'):
            return answer
        print(f'  {answer!r} is not test, publish or quit: type one of them '
              'exactly, or press Enter to stop.')


def main(argv: list[str] | None = None) -> int:
    argv = sys.argv[1:] if argv is None else list(argv)
    if '--branch-plan' in argv:
        try:
            if len(argv) > 1:
                raise BlessError(
                    '--branch-plan takes no other argument: it reports this '
                    'tree\'s branch plan and exits, without a mode and '
                    'without the gate')
            print(json.dumps(plan_report(), indent=2))
        except BlessError as exc:
            print(f'bless: refused: {exc}', file=sys.stderr)
            return 1
        return 0

    parser = argparse.ArgumentParser(
        prog='bless',
        description='Run every gate on this tree, then ask what to do with a '
                    'tree that passed: "test" rehearses the upload on '
                    'TestPyPI, "publish" tags, uploads to PyPI and pushes.')
    try:
        leftover = parser.parse_known_args(argv)[1]
        if leftover:
            raise BlessError(
                f'bless takes no arguments, so {leftover[0]!r} is not a mode.\n'
                'The gate always runs first and then asks, so run ./bless and '
                'type test or publish at the prompt.')
        version = read_version()
        say('bless', f'{NAME} {version}')
        facts = preflight(version)
        say('preflight', f'clean tree at {facts["sha"]} on {facts["branch"]}')
        public = check_index(version)
        gates()
        wheel, sdist = build(version)
        inspect_dist(wheel, sdist)
        check(wheel, sdist)
        smoke(wheel, version)
        plan = branch_stage(facts['branch'], PROJECT['branches'])
        checklist(version, facts, public, wheel, sdist, plan)

        mode = ask_mode()
        if not mode:
            say('stop', 'nothing was tagged, uploaded or pushed')
            return 0
        if mode == 'test':
            rehearse(version)
            return 0

        align_branches(plan, facts['branch'])
        tag(version)
        upload(wheel, sdist, version)
        references = push_references(facts['branch'], version, plan['release'])
        verify_index(version, wheel, advice=(
            f'The tag v{version} exists locally and PyPI has both files, so '
            'only the push is left:\n'
            f'  git push origin {" ".join(references)}'))
        publish_git(references)
    except BlessError as exc:
        print(f'bless: refused: {exc}', file=sys.stderr)
        return 1

    branches = PROJECT['branches']
    say('done', f'{NAME} {version} is published and {_joined(branches)} '
                 f'{"are" if len(branches) > 1 else "is"} tagged')
    return 0


if __name__ == '__main__':
    sys.exit(main())
